      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-17128",
        "knowledge_point_id": "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle",
        "problem": "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$",
        "worked_solution": "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$",
        "answer_fields": [
