        "math_academy_id": "q-7175",
        "knowledge_point_id": "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment",
        "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{5t-10}{t},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5t-10}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$",
